faq:
  - q: "What are the dominant industries in Massachusetts?"
    a: "Massachusetts has one of the most educated and high-income workforces in the US, concentrated in the Greater Boston area. Dominant sectors: (1) Biotech and Life Sciences — Greater Boston (Kendall Square in Cambridge specifically) is the world's premier biotech cluster. Moderna, Biogen, Vertex Pharmaceuticals, Takeda US, and hundreds of biotech startups are clustered within a few square miles. The Broad Institute, MIT, and Harvard create a continuous research-to-company pipeline. (2) Higher Education and Healthcare — Massachusetts has the highest concentration of research universities in the US (Harvard, MIT, Tufts, Northeastern, Boston University, BC, Brandeis). These institutions and their affiliated hospitals (Mass General, Brigham and Women's, Beth Israel Deaconess) employ hundreds of thousands and spin out enormous economic activity. (3) Financial Services — Boston is the second-largest mutual fund market in the US. Fidelity Investments, State Street, Liberty Mutual, and Wellington Management are headquartered here. (4) Technology — Boston and Cambridge have a growing tech ecosystem. HubSpot, Wayfair, Chewy, and dozens of enterprise software companies are headquartered in or near Boston."
  - q: "How does the Boston biotech job market work and how do you break into it?"
    a: "Kendall Square in Cambridge, Massachusetts is the most competitive biotech job market in the world. Breaking in: (1) Educational credentials matter significantly — PhDs from MIT, Harvard, and leading research universities are the primary talent pipeline for scientific roles. For non-scientific roles (business development, program management, regulatory affairs), professional experience in adjacent fields (consulting, healthcare, finance) plus genuine biotech interest is the path. (2) The network is tight-knit — the Boston biotech community is small enough that personal connections carry enormous weight. MIT Enterprise Forum, MassBio events, and the Broad Institute community are key networking venues. (3) Understand the drug development process — candidates who cannot discuss the IND-to-NDA development pipeline, clinical trial phases, and FDA approval processes are at a disadvantage for any non-engineering business role in biotech. (4) Compensation is strong but structured differently from tech — base salary is often lower than equivalent seniority tech roles, but bonus programs, stock options (pre-IPO), and benefits are substantial. Understanding biotech compensation vs. tech compensation requires detailed modeling."
  - q: "What is Boston's financial services job market like?"
    a: "Boston is a major financial center focused on asset management, rather than investment banking like New York. Key dynamics: (1) Asset management is dominant — Fidelity Investments (~75,000 employees globally, significant Boston presence), State Street Corporation, Wellington Management, GMO, Putnam, and MFS are all headquartered in Boston. This creates unique demand for investment analysts, portfolio managers, quantitative researchers, and operations professionals. (2) Mutual fund and ETF operations — Boston's asset management concentration creates enormous demand for operations, compliance, transfer agency, and fund accounting professionals that is unique to this market. (3) Insurance — Liberty Mutual, Hanover Insurance Group, and John Hancock (Manulife) create a significant insurance employer cluster. (4) Investment banking is present but secondary — Goldman Sachs, JPMorgan, and other bulge brackets have Boston offices but the investment banking community is smaller than New York. Biotech IPO and life sciences advisory banking is a Boston specialty."
  - q: "What does the Boston tech ecosystem look like?"
    a: "Boston has a growing but distinct tech ecosystem: (1) Enterprise software dominates — HubSpot (CRM/marketing), Drift (conversational marketing, acquired), Salsify (product experience), and dozens of B2B SaaS companies are headquartered in Boston. Consumer tech is less prominent than in SF. (2) Academic institution spin-outs are frequent — MIT, Harvard, and Northeastern produce a continuous flow of deep-tech startups in AI, robotics, quantum computing, and biotech. MassRobotics, the MIT spinout ecosystem, and Harvard Innovation Labs are notable. (3) Defense tech is growing — Boston's concentration of research universities and DARPA relationships creates unique defense technology companies (including AI and autonomy startups). (4) Compensation is below SF and NYC tech but above the national average. Massachusetts has an income tax (5%) that affects after-tax comparison with no-income-tax states. (5) The Route 128 / I-95 tech corridor (Waltham, Lexington, Burlington) hosts many large tech company offices alongside Boston-based companies."
  - q: "How should Massachusetts job seekers approach salary negotiation?"
    a: "Massachusetts salary negotiation has several specific considerations: (1) Massachusetts has a 5% flat income tax (plus 4% surtax on income over $1M under the 2022 millionaire's tax). This is relevant when comparing MA offers to no-income-tax states (Texas, Florida, Washington, Nevada). (2) Biotech compensation modeling requires understanding the full package — base salary, annual bonus, stock options (pre-IPO or post-IPO public stock), RSUs, and sign-on components. Biotech stock options with 10-year exercise windows and 4-year vesting can be extraordinarily valuable or worthless depending on the company's trajectory. Always ask for the strike price, the most recent 409A valuation, and the fully diluted share count. (3) Asset management compensation is heavily deferred — bonus amounts and timing are specific to each firm's structure. Understanding when and how bonuses are paid is critical for cash flow planning. (4) Boston biotech companies are often acquired — understanding change-of-control provisions in employment agreements is important for pre-IPO biotech employees."
